---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const desc = 'Free AI energy and carbon estimator: turn GPU hours into kilowatt hours and CO2e using datacenter PUE and a grid emissions factor you choose.';
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'AI Energy and Carbon Estimator', applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Web', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  url: 'https://theworldofai.org/calculators/energy-carbon/',
});
---
<Base title="AI Energy and Carbon Estimator: kWh and CO2e from GPU hours — The World of AI" description={desc} current="/calculators/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <h1>AI Energy and Carbon Estimator</h1>
  <p class="answer">The energy a training or inference run consumes is <b>GPU power times hours
    times PUE</b>, where PUE is the datacenter's power usage effectiveness, the multiplier that
    accounts for cooling and everything else in the building. Emissions are that figure times
    the carbon intensity of the grid supplying it, which varies by more than tenfold between
    regions and is the single largest lever on the answer.</p>

  <div class="calc">
    <label>GPU hours <input type="number" id="hours" value="10000" step="100" min="0" /></label>
    <label>GPU power draw (watts)
      <select id="watts">
        <option value="700" selected>H100 SXM, 700 W</option>
        <option value="400">A100 80GB, 400 W</option>
        <option value="1000">B200 class, 1000 W</option>
        <option value="300">Workstation GPU, 300 W</option>
      </select>
    </label>
    <label>Datacenter PUE <input type="number" id="pue" value="1.2" step="0.05" min="1" max="3" /></label>
    <label>Grid carbon intensity (g CO2e per kWh)
      <select id="grid">
        <option value="369" selected>United States average, about 369</option>
        <option value="230">European Union average, about 230</option>
        <option value="30">Low-carbon grid, hydro or nuclear heavy, about 30</option>
        <option value="580">Coal-heavy grid, about 580</option>
        <option value="0">Contracted zero-carbon supply</option>
      </select>
    </label>
    <label>Electricity price (USD per kWh) <input type="number" id="price" value="0.08" step="0.01" min="0" /></label>
    <div class="out" id="out" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <h2>How the math works and where the numbers come from</h2>
  <p>Energy in kWh is watts ÷ 1000 × GPU hours × PUE. Emissions are kWh × grid intensity. The
    GPU wattages are the manufacturers' published board power figures; a real run averages
    somewhat below peak, so the result skews conservative. PUE of 1.2 is typical of a modern
    hyperscale facility, while older enterprise datacenters run 1.5 to 2.0.</p>
  <p>The grid intensity presets are rounded regional averages and are the weakest number here.
    Real intensity swings hour by hour with what is on the grid, so a run scheduled overnight in
    a wind-heavy region can differ several-fold from the same run at a summer peak. If you need
    a defensible figure, use your provider's own regional data rather than an average, and say
    which you used.</p>
  <p>Excluded: embodied carbon in manufacturing the hardware, networking and storage outside the
    GPU, and the water footprint of cooling. Each is a live research area and none is trivial.</p>

  <div class="cite-box">Cite this tool: <code>"AI Energy and Carbon Estimator." The World of AI,
    theworldofai.org/calculators/energy-carbon/.</code></div>

  <script is:inline>
  (function () {
    function fmt(n, d) { return n.toLocaleString(undefined, { maximumFractionDigits: d === undefined ? 0 : d }); }
    function calc() {
      var h = parseFloat(document.getElementById('hours').value) || 0;
      var w = parseFloat(document.getElementById('watts').value);
      var pue = parseFloat(document.getElementById('pue').value) || 1;
      var g = parseFloat(document.getElementById('grid').value);
      var price = parseFloat(document.getElementById('price').value) || 0;

      var kwh = (w / 1000) * h * pue;
      var kg = (kwh * g) / 1000;
      var cost = kwh * price;
      var homes = kwh / 10500; // US average household annual electricity use
      var miles = kg / 0.4;    // approx kg CO2e per mile, average petrol car

      document.getElementById('out').innerHTML =
        '<b>' + fmt(kwh) + ' kWh</b> and <b>' + fmt(kg) + ' kg CO2e</b><br>' +
        '$' + fmt(cost) + ' of electricity at the price entered.<br>' +
        'For scale, that is about ' + fmt(homes, 2) + ' US households\u2019 annual electricity use' +
        (g > 0 ? ', and roughly the emissions of driving ' + fmt(miles) + ' miles.' : '.') +
        '<br><span class="note">Excludes embodied carbon, networking, storage, and cooling water.</span>';
    }
    ['hours', 'watts', 'pue', 'grid', 'price'].forEach(function (id) {
      document.getElementById(id).addEventListener('input', calc);
      document.getElementById(id).addEventListener('change', calc);
    });
    calc();
  })();
  </script>
</Base>
